            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did burada mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: నిప్పు",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "నిప్పు (nippu) — \"fire, a live coal\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "నిప్పు (nippu) is inherited, and it is the fire you can point at: the live coal, the burning thing in the hearth. అగ్ని, borrowed from Sanskrit, is fire as an idea and as a god, the one that carries offerings."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So Telugu keeps a working fire and a ceremonial one, in the same way it keeps a పొద్దు and a సూర్యుడు. That split has turned up so often in these chapters that you can start expecting it: the old word does the work, the borrowed one does the ceremony."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "నిప్పు (nippu) is what a house is banked down around at night, and putting it out is the last thing done before sleep. A good place to say వెళ్ళొస్తాను."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: గాలి (gāli), మంచు (mañcu), ఇసుక (isuka), బురద (burada), నిప్పు (nippu). Weather enough to take your leave on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
